# Inference

Inference is performed using XTTS v2 in conditioning mode.

No weights are updated during this stage.
All voice adaptation occurs through reference audio embeddings.

---

## Conditioning-Based Generation

XTTS supports speaker conditioning by extracting embeddings from one or more
reference audio clips.

During inference:

1. Reference audio clips are encoded into speaker embeddings
2. Embeddings are aggregated internally by the model
3. Generated speech is conditioned on the resulting speaker representation

This approach enables voice adaptation without fine-tuning.

---

## Single vs Multi-Reference Conditioning

Two conditioning setups were evaluated:

### Single-reference conditioning

- Uses one short reference clip
- Fast and lightweight
- Often produces generic or unstable speaker identity

### Multi-reference conditioning

- Uses multiple reference clips (5–10)
- Embeddings are averaged internally
- Produces stronger identity consistency

Multi-reference conditioning consistently outperformed single-reference usage.

---

## Reference Composition Experiments

Different reference sets were constructed from processed audio:

- Neutral reference set
- Expressive reference set
- Mixed reference set

Each reference set was used with identical text input to isolate the effect of
reference composition.

Observed differences included:

- pacing variation
- pitch consistency
- expressiveness
- stability during longer outputs

This demonstrates that conditioning behavior is strongly influenced by
reference audio selection.

---

## Long-Form Generation

Longer narration samples were generated to evaluate:

- speaker drift
- identity stability
- fatigue artifacts
- tonal flattening

While conditioning improves short-form similarity, long-form generation still
exhibits gradual degradation — a known limitation of reference-based methods.

---

## Summary

Inference experiments demonstrate that:

- reference quality matters more than reference quantity
- multiple short, clean clips outperform single long samples
- conditioning can approximate speaker identity without training
- expressive control remains limited without model fine-tuning
